   "id": "pmt/pure-core-2/ch6/q8",
   "question": "<ol class=\"parts\" type=\"a\"><li><p>Use a hyperbolic substitution and calculus to show that</p><div class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\int\\frac{x^2}{\\sqrt{x^2-1}}\\,\\mathrm dx=\\frac12\\left[x\\sqrt{x^2-1}+\\operatorname{arcosh}x\\right]+k\" data-display=\"true\"></div><p>where <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k\"></span> is an arbitrary constant.</p><span class=\"marks\">(6)</span></li></ol><figure class=\"practice-figure\" style=\"max-width:70mm;margin:2mm auto\"><img src=\"resources/pmt/pure-core-2-ch6/q08-fig-1.png\" alt=\"Figure 1: axes x and y with origin O; the curve C rising from the x-axis to the right of O; the region R under C between the curve, the x-axis and the vertical line x = 3 is shaded\"></figure><p>Figure 1 shows a sketch of part of the curve <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"C\"></span> with equation</p><div class=\"equation\" data-math=\"y=\\frac{4}{15}x\\operatorname{arcosh}x\\qquad x\\geqslant1\" data-display=\"true\"></div><p>The finite region <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"R\"></span>, shown shaded in Figure 1, is bounded by the curve <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"C\"></span>, the <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x\"></span>-axis and the line with equation <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x=3\"></span></p><ol class=\"parts\" type=\"a\" start=\"2\"><li><p>Using algebraic integration and the result from part (a), show that the area of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"R\"></span> is given by</p><div class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\frac{1}{15}\\left[17\\ln\\left(3+2\\sqrt2\\right)-6\\sqrt2\\right]\" data-display=\"true\"></div><span class=\"marks\">(5)</span></li></ol>",
